  "summaryEn": "<p>One early morning at Gandhiji's ashram in Wardha, a little boy walks in. His clothes are torn and he is very tired. To everyone's questions he gives just one answer: “I must see Bapu right now!”</p><p>Gandhiji, sitting on a small mat spinning thread, calls the boy close. The boy cries: his father and mother are gone and he has no one. Gandhiji tells him the ashram is his home now. But the boy is worried: “I don't know how to do any work. How can I stay here doing nothing?”</p><p>Gandhiji kisses him with proud tears and says Mother India has room to give work to every one of her children. If little hands become strong enough to wipe away the country's tears, there is no sorrow left. Big idea: everyone, even a small child, can do useful work.</p><p>Bonus: a short true-style story from the book about a boy so busy making chapatis that he didn't notice Gandhiji walk past — and Gandhiji said that boy was the only one who truly “saw” him.</p>",
